.TH TROFF 1
.SH NAME
troff \- typeset text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B troff
[
.BI -m x
] [
.BI -r x = y
] [
.BI -d x = y
] [
.B -C
] [
.BI -T dev
] [
.BI -F dir
] [
.BI -M dir
] [
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Troff
sets the text of its
.I files
(or of its standard input) in type, as their requests and escapes
direct, and writes on its standard output a description of the pages
it made, independent of any device, for
.IR tr2pdf (1)
to make a PDF of, or for the document views
.RI ( wm/page (1),
Xenith, Lucifer) to show as it is.
It is a translation of Ali Gholami Rudi's Neatroff:
its language is Neatroff's, which is troff's with long names
.RB ( \e*[name] ,
.BR \en[name] ),
OpenType's features and kerning, a paragraph filled at once
(as TeX fills one), and hyphenation by TeX's patterns
(English and Ancient Greek here).
For the same input and fonts, on a device with no
.B sizescale
(below), its output is, byte for byte, Neatroff's, but where it does
as troff did (Plan 9's, groff's), below.
.PP
Its preprocessors
.IR eqn (1),
.IR tbl (1),
.IR pic (1)
and
.IR refer (1)
set mathematics, tables, pictures and citations;
.IR book (1)
runs them all, and
.I troff
as often as a book's references need.
The macro packages are described in
.IR troff (6).
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.BI -m x
Read the macro package
.BI tmac. x
(from
.BR /lib/tmac )
before the files:
.B -ms
for papers,
.B -man
for manual pages,
.B -mbook
for books.
.TP
.BI -r x = y
Set the number register
.I x
to
.IR y .
.TP
.BI -d x = y
Define the string
.I x
as
.IR y .
.TP
.B -C
Compatibility: names of two characters, run into what follows them,
as old troff reads them.
.TP
.BI -T dev
Set for the device
.I dev
(the default,
.BR utf :
Libertinus, its Serif, Sans, Mono and Math, and DejaVu Sans for what
they lack).
.TP
.BI -F dir
Read the device's description from
.I dir
(default
.BR /lib/troff ).
.TP
.BI -M dir
Read macro packages from
.I dir
(default
.BR /lib/tmac ).
.SS "Not Neatroff's"
Where Neatroff departs from troff (Plan 9's, groff's, Bell's) and a
document written now would be set wrongly, this troff does as troff
does:
.TP
.BR \es10 " to " \es39
are sizes of two digits (Neatroff reads one, but in compatibility mode).
.TP
.B .na
sets lines from the left margin, whatever
.B .ad
said
.RB ( .ad
gives the alignment back); Neatroff kept a right or a centred one.
.TP
.B \en(.j
is troff's number for the adjustment: 0 left (or
.BR .na ),
1 both margins, 3 centre, 5 right, 2 and 4 centre and right under
.BR .na ;
Neatroff's own modes (whole paragraphs, and the rest) above them, from
32, so that
.B .ad \en(.j
restores them too.
.IR tbl 's
.B ".if \en(.j=0 .na"
and -ms's
.B ".ad \en(SJ"
read it so.
.TP
.B .rules
is on (below): rules are drawn as lines.
.TP
.B \ef(CW
classic troff's constant width, is this troff's
.BR CR ;
with
.B -C
also Plan 9's
.B H
.RB ( HR )
and -man's
.B L
.RB ( CR ).
A name no font has that classic troff's documents use is its
character:
.B \e(Do
.RB ( $ ),
.B \e(tm
(™),
.B \e(~=
(≃),
.B \e(sq
(□),
.B \e(bx
(■),
.B \e(ob
(◦),
.B \e(lh
(☜),
.B \e(rh
(☞).
.TP
.BI ".cflags " "n c ..."
groff's: with 4 in
.IR n ,
a line may break after each of the characters
.I c
(as after
.BR \e: );
without it, not.
The other flags are not this troff's.
.TP
.BI \eD't " n" '
The lines drawn after it are
.I n
units thick (\eD't 0' the default again); Neatroff's dropped it.
.TP
.B \e-
A minus sign, read (searched, copied) as a hyphen-minus, so that an
option copied from a page is one a shell takes.
.PP
These are this troff's own; with none of them requested,
it sets as Neatroff sets.
.TP
.BI .mtp " n"
Protrusion: with
.I n
not zero, punctuation, hyphens and some letters at a line's ends hang
into the margins, each by the thousandths of its width
.B .mtpc
gives it, so that the text's edges look straight.
.TP
.BI ".mtpc " "font left right chars ..."
Set the protrusion of
.IR chars ,
in
.IR font ,
at a line's left and its right.
.TP
.BI ".mte " "stretch shrink step"
Expansion: a justified line's glyphs may be widened or narrowed, by up
to
.I stretch
and
.I shrink
thousandths in steps of
.IR step ,
so that its spaces vary less; tr2pdf draws them so.
.TP
.BI .wo " n"
Widows and orphans: with
.I n
not zero, a paragraph is broken by a page only with two of its lines
at least on either side.
.TP
.BI .rules " n"
Rules drawn: with
.I n
not zero (or none), the
.B \el
and
.B \eL
of rule characters
.RB ( \e(ul ,
.BR \e(ru ,
.BR \e(rn ,
.BR \e(br ...)
are drawn, each a rectangle where its glyphs' ink would be, not set as
glyphs side by side; on unless
.B ".rules 0"
(Neatroff's) says not.
A box rule
.RB ( \e(br ,
tbl's sides) meets the underrules
.RB ( \e(ul ,
tbl's rules) at their outer edges: tbl ends it 2% of an em below a
row's baseline, where Bell's devices drew
.BR \e(ul ,
and Libertinus's is deeper.
.TP
.BI .mg " n"
With
.I n
not zero, a character no font has is said on the standard error
(once), not dropped without a word.
.TP
.BI .imgsize " file"
An image's size in pixels and its resolution in dots to the inch (0 if
it says none), from its header (a PNG's, a JPEG's), or a PDF's first
page's in tenths of a point (720 to the inch; its crop box, or media
box), in the registers
.BR imgw ,
.B imgh
and
.BR imgres ;
all 0 if it cannot be read.
.TP
.B \en[.pend]
The lines the text not yet set will take, were the paragraph to end
here.
.TP
.B \en[.n]
Classic troff's: the length of the text on the line set last (not its
indent).
.TP
.BI \eX'nr " name value" '
Set the register
.I name
to
.I value
as the line it is in goes out (not when it is read): what is set on a
page, known at the page's foot.
.TP
.BI sizescale " n"
In a device's
.BR DESC ,
as groff's: sizes in
.RI 1/ n
points (scaled points), so that
.B ".ps 8.8"
and
.B \es[8.8]
are 8.8 points, not 9; the unit
.B z
is a scaled point,
.B \en[.ps]
the size in them, and
.B \en(.s
the size in whole points, as ever.
Its output's sizes
.RB ( s )
are in scaled points.
.BR devutf 's
is 100.
.PP
A pair is kerned as its font kerns it, once: a font's kerning lookup
that its scripts share is performed once, not once for each script.
.SH EXAMPLES
A paper, as a PDF:
.IP
.EX
troff -ms paper.ms | tr2pdf >paper.pdf
.EE
.PP
Its tables and equations first:
.IP
.EX
tbl paper.ms | eqn | troff -ms | tr2pdf >paper.pdf
.EE
